// SITUATION: During a college project building an e-commerce backend,
// my teammate wanted to store all order data in a single orders table
// with JSON columns for flexibility. I believed we should normalize
// the schema with separate tables for order_items and customer_addresses.

// TASK: As the person assigned to design the database schema,
// I needed to make a decision that both of us could support.

// ACTION: I scheduled a 30-minute call to understand his reasoning.
// He was concerned about query simplicity and avoiding complex joins.
// I acknowledged that was valid for simple cases, then showed him:

var orderReport = """
    SELECT o.id, o.total, c.name
    FROM orders o
    JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id
    WHERE o.status = 'COMPLETED'
    AND o.created_at > '2024-01-01';
    """;

// I explained that SQL joins are optimized and readable, while
// JSON querying in SQL is messy and hard to index. We agreed to
// normalize core entities but use a JSON field for optional metadata.

// RESULT: The schema performed well, queries stayed clean, and we
// both learned that hybrid approaches can satisfy multiple concerns.
// We documented the decision in our project README so future
// contributors understood the rationale.
