// SITUATION: In a hackathon team, one member never responded to
// our Slack messages but would randomly push code without discussion.
// His commits broke our integration tests multiple times.

// What I did first (empathy): I noticed he was online late at night
// based on commit timestamps, different from our daytime working hours.
// Maybe he had classes during the day or was in a different timezone.

// Direct communication: I sent him a DM (not in the group channel):
var message = """
    Hey, I see you're working late nights—that's dedication!
    I noticed some commits are breaking tests. Can we sync
    for 15 mins tomorrow so I understand your approach?
    I want to make sure we're not duplicating work.
    """;

// What I learned: He was working a part-time job during the day
// and coding at night when he was exhausted. He preferred seeing
// working code rather than long design discussions in Slack.

// Solution: We agreed he'd create draft PRs instead of pushing
// directly to main, and I'd review them async with comments.
// He could code his way, I could review on my schedule.

// RESULT: The remaining two days went smoothly, we finished the
// project, and I learned that async code review is more inclusive
// than expecting everyone to attend synchronous planning meetings.
